# Solador metrics agent: the daily unattended update timer (user unit, #394).
#
# Installed verbatim by deploy/install.sh --enable-timer beside
# solador-agent-update.service (there is nothing to render). Present only on a
# host whose operator opted in.
#
# The cadence is the decision recorded on #394: DAILY, NO CATCH-UP. Once per
# 24 hours while this user's manager is running; never at enable, boot, login
# or wake to make up for an interval that was missed; a missed check is
# discarded, not replayed and not coalesced into one delayed run.
#
# That is why these are MONOTONIC timers and not OnCalendar=daily:
#
#   * OnActiveSec=24h — the first firing is 24 h after the timer unit itself
#     starts, so `enable --now` (and the manager starting at boot under
#     linger, or at login without it) never fires immediately, and a session
#     restart begins a fresh interval.
#   * OnUnitActiveSec=24h — every later firing is 24 h after the update
#     service last STARTED, whatever it exited with, so a failed run is not
#     retried until tomorrow.
#   * The monotonic clock pauses while the system is suspended
#     (systemd.timer(5), "OnActiveSec=…: If the computer is temporarily
#     suspended, the monotonic clock generally pauses, too"), so a wake
#     cannot find an elapsed deadline waiting. An OnCalendar= timer keys off
#     the realtime clock and DOES fire on resume when its time passed during
#     sleep — the coalesced wake-time check this cadence forbids — and
#     Persistent= only governs missed firings across a full stop of the
#     manager. WakeSystem= stays off for the same reason: it would swap in
#     the clock that keeps counting through suspend.
#
# What the clock does NOT hold, and what holds it instead (#411). A
# `daemon-reload` (every installer re-run does one) re-bases OnActiveSec to
# the reload time — a first check that has not yet fired moves later, never
# sooner — and a source trace of systemd v255's timer.c found that after the
# first day a reload leaves that one-shot re-armed, and the CLOCK_REALTIME
# change the next resume delivers recomputes its deadline from the timer's
# ORIGINAL activation, in the past: one firing seconds after wake. Nil on a
# server that never sleeps; real on an opted-in laptop; not observed on a
# real suspend-capable user manager (nothing in this repo runs one). So the
# oneshot this timer starts carries an ExecCondition= guard,
# ~/.local/bin/solador-agent-update-guard, that applies the cadence to every
# firing however the manager arrived at it: a firing within five minutes of
# the last resume (or boot), or within 23 h of the last attempt, is skipped
# cleanly with its reason in the journal; a guard that cannot read its
# inputs fails the unit rather than guess. The timer file is what it was —
# the guard is the property's enforcement, not a change to its schedule.
#
# The launchd counterpart is app.solador.agent.update.plist, whose
# StartInterval carries the same 24 h, with the launcher's update mode
# standing where the guard stands here.

[Unit]
Description=Solador metrics agent: daily unattended update check

[Timer]
OnActiveSec=24h
OnUnitActiveSec=24h

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
